---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/portfolio/PageHeader.astro';
import MetaLabel from '../../components/editorial/MetaLabel.astro';
import ArchitectureAnnotation from '../../components/editorial/ArchitectureAnnotation.astro';
import EvidenceObject from '../../components/editorial/EvidenceObject.astro';
import SectionBreak from '../../components/editorial/SectionBreak.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Archive: the Soft Architecture design system | Karan Mittal"
  description="A historical record of the design system that preceded Instrumented Editorial."
  robots="noindex, follow"
>
  <main class="container case-layout">
    <aside class="archive-notice" aria-label="Archived design-system notice">
      <span>Archived system / superseded</span>
      <p>This page documents the earlier Soft Architecture direction. The active site now uses Instrumented Editorial; this record remains only to preserve the design history.</p>
      <a href="/studio/components/">Inspect the current component system →</a>
    </aside>
    <PageHeader
      label="Case study"
      title="Rebuilding this site on a written design contract"
      description="Every design decision on this page is verifiable in the repository itself. That is the point: the site documents its own construction the way a system should document its own architecture."
    />

    <div class="case-body">
      <section class="cs-section" aria-labelledby="cs-ambiguity">
        <MetaLabel index="01" label="Ambiguity" />
        <h2 id="cs-ambiguity">The brief was a feeling.</h2>
        <p>
          The original direction for this site was <em>warm editorial</em>: soft serif display type,
          cream paper tones, generous italic pull quotes. It photographed well and it was wrong.
          The problem was never articulable as a bug list &mdash; the pages were individually pleasant and
          collectively incoherent. Three pages described three different engineers.
        </p>
        <p>
          The actual uncertainty: nobody could state, in one sentence, what visual fact made a page
          feel like <em>this engineer</em> rather than a template. Until that was stated, every component
          was a coin flip.
        </p>
      </section>

      <SectionBreak />

      <section class="cs-section" aria-labelledby="cs-constraint">
        <MetaLabel index="02" label="Constraint" />
        <h2 id="cs-constraint">What made it difficult</h2>
        <ArchitectureAnnotation index="02" label="CONSTRAINT" consequence="the spec became the test oracle">
          The design language existed as a 2,885-line written specification before implementation
          resumed &#8212; palette, type scale, density rhythm, diagram grammar, dark-mode policy,
          anti-patterns, acceptance criteria.
        </ArchitectureAnnotation>
        <ul>
          <li>The stack was fixed: Astro static output on GitHub Pages; no server runtime, no client framework.</li>
          <li>Existing essays and exhibits had to survive the migration without content rewrites.</li>
          <li>The spec bans what most portfolios reach for first: site-wide dark mode, hover scale, decorative topology, neon gradients (§34).</li>
          <li>One documented deviation allowed: Inter stands in for Google Sans Flex (licensing and self-hosting weight), recorded in the token file header.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <SectionBreak />

      <section class="cs-section" aria-labelledby="cs-model">
        <MetaLabel index="03" label="Model" />
        <h2 id="cs-model">Colour is state, not decoration</h2>
        <p>
          The mental model that made the problem manageable: treat the palette as a
          <strong>type system for meaning</strong>. Blue is structure. Pink is attention &mdash;
          one node per composition, never a default CTA. Lavender is inferred or secondary.
          Ink planes are conclusions. If a colour cannot say what it <em>means</em>, it does not ship.
        </p>
        <p>
          The second half of the model is the density rhythm. Pages move through
          <code>quiet &rarr; explanation &rarr; complexity &rarr; evidence &rarr; quiet</code>.
          Visual noise is concentrated where the idea is systemic and stripped everywhere else.
          This single rule settled most of the arguments the warm-editorial direction kept losing.
        </p>
      </section>

      <SectionBreak />

      <section class="cs-section" aria-labelledby="cs-system">
        <MetaLabel index="04" label="System" />
        <h2 id="cs-system">What was built</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>A semantic token layer (<code>--blue-500</code>, <code>--signal-warm</code>, <code>--lavender-400</code>, spacing, radii, shadows) with zero raw hex values outside the token definitions.</li>
          <li>A component kit in four folders: <code>editorial/</code> (Exhibit, MetaLabel, EvidenceObject, Annotation, DarkPlane, TintedPlane), <code>chrome/</code>, <code>diagram/</code>, <code>portfolio/</code>.</li>
          <li>An exhibit render pipeline that re-skins Excalidraw exports into the Soft Architecture palette at build time &mdash; light-only exports, because darkness is semantic (§21).</li>
          <li>A specimen page (<a href="/studio/components/">/studio/components</a>) that exercises every primitive before pages multiply.</li>
          <li>This case-study spine, applied to itself.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <SectionBreak />

      <section class="cs-section" aria-labelledby="cs-decision">
        <MetaLabel index="05" label="Decision" />
        <h2 id="cs-decision">What was deliberately not built</h2>
        <p>
          A theme lab shipped early: three full directions, each with light and dark token sets,
          switchable from the studio page. It was removed in the same migration that finished the rest.
        </p>
        <ArchitectureAnnotation index="05" label="DECISION" consequence="one language, maintained">
          Three themes means three palettes to keep accessible, three diagram skins to re-render,
          and three chances to dilute the identity. The lab's real output was the decision it forced:
          pick one direction and delete the rest.
        </ArchitectureAnnotation>
        <p>
          Also rejected: Tailwind (the spec mandates semantic tokens over utility classes &mdash;
          §27.1), per-page dark variants, a contact form (§23.7: the homepage dark plane is canonical),
          and a standalone /now page (folded into About).
        </p>
      </section>

      <SectionBreak />

      <section class="cs-section" aria-labelledby="cs-evidence">
        <MetaLabel index="06" label="Evidence" />
        <h2 id="cs-evidence">What changed</h2>
        <div class="evidence-grid">
          <EvidenceObject
            index="01"
            label="Tokens"
            metric="0"
            method="dangling or undocumented token references"
            outcome="grep-verified across all source files"
          />
          <EvidenceObject
            index="02"
            label="Regression"
            metric="18"
            method="full-page baselines × 375/768/1440"
            outcome="Playwright suite, deterministic across runs"
          />
          <EvidenceObject
            index="03"
            label="Static QA"
            metric="0"
            method="warnings from the studio preflight"
            outcome="raw hexes, dead tokens, SVG typography all resolved"
          />
        </div>
        <p>
          Primary sources: the migration commit chain in this repository's history, the
          <a href="/studio/">studio control room</a>, the preflight script
          (<code>scripts/studio-check.js</code>), and the visual baselines under
          <code>tests/visual/__screenshots__/</code>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <SectionBreak />

      <section class="cs-section" aria-labelledby="cs-aftermath">
        <MetaLabel index="07" label="Aftermath" />
        <h2 id="cs-aftermath">What I would do differently</h2>
        <p>
          The specimen-first milestone (spec §40) arrived late. Components were built for real pages
          before a single place exercised them in isolation, which is why the theme lab existed at all
          &mdash; it was a makeshift specimen. Built today, the specimen page comes first and the lab
          never ships.
        </p>
        <p>
          Still open, deliberately: diagrams are hand-coded SVG rather than data-driven primitives
          (spec §28 wants nodes and edges as typed data); social derivative cards are designed but not
          generated (§31). Both are queued behind the same principle that killed the theme lab:
          do not multiply artefacts until the language holds.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  .case-layout {
    max-width: 720px;
  }
  .archive-notice {
    margin: 2rem 0 3rem;
    padding: 1rem 0 1.15rem;
    border-top: 2px solid var(--ie-rust);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ie-rule-strong);
  }
  .archive-notice span {
    color: var(--ie-rust);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--ie-type-label);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.07em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .archive-notice p {
    margin: 0.7rem 0;
    color: var(--ie-ink-secondary);
  }
  .archive-notice a {
    color: var(--ie-blue);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--ie-type-label);
  }
  .case-body {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }
  .cs-section h2 {
    margin: var(--space-2) 0 var(--space-4);
    font-size: clamp(var(--text-xl), 3vw, var(--text-2xl));
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }
  .cs-section p {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
    max-width: 66ch;
  }
  .cs-section ul {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-4);
    padding-left: var(--space-5);
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }
  .cs-section li {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
  }
  .cs-section code {
    padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
    background: var(--blue-100);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.85em;
    color: var(--ink-900);
  }
  .evidence-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-4);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-5);
  }
</style>
